<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
          http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
          https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.23.xsd">

    <!--
      2026-09-23: "ZAPISI PARTIJA" — the full replay of a finished online game,
      stored as one JSON document (game/README.md §8.8, game/BOT.md).

      WHY. The bot is tuned against hand-written scenarios and self-play, both
      of which only ever show it positions somebody already thought of. The
      owner's request: keep whole games people actually played, so a bad
      decision can be found after the fact and turned into a scenario test.

      ONE ROW PER GAME, one-to-one with `game_results`:

        * `game_result_id` is UNIQUE and ON DELETE CASCADE. Unique because the
          reporter sends the replay INSIDE the result POST — same request,
          same idempotency key (`game_results.uuid`), so a retry can never
          produce a second replay for the same game. Cascade because a replay
          of a deleted result is an orphan with no meaning; it also makes
          `ops/reset-game-stats.sh` correct without naming a third table in
          the TRUNCATE (it names it anyway, so the intent is readable).

        * `replay` is jsonb, not a normalised deal/trick/play tree. Exactly
          the `blok_sessions.payload` reasoning: the document is written once,
          read whole, and never queried by a field inside a deal. Three more
          tables would have bought zero queries. Anything a LISTING needs —
          when, which bot, how big — is a real column.

        * `bot_version` is denormalised OUT of the document on purpose: it is
          the one field the export filters and groups by ("all games played
          by the 2026-09-23 bot"), and a b-tree on a real column beats a jsonb
          expression index for a question asked that often.

        * `size_bytes` is what the reporter sent, recorded so the owner can
          see the archive's growth without summing `length(replay::text)`
          over the whole table.

      NO GIN INDEX, deliberately. Nothing searches INSIDE the JSON: the export
      is "give me the last N replays" and the analysis happens outside the
      database. A GIN index on a document this size would cost write time and
      a lot of space for a query nobody makes. `created_at` gets a plain
      b-tree, which is what both readers actually use — the newest-first
      export and the retention sweep.

      RETENTION. `GameReplayRetentionJob` deletes rows older than
      `game.replays.retention-days` (default 180) nightly. The replays are
      research material with a shelf life — a game played against a bot that
      no longer exists teaches nothing — and this is also what keeps the
      table's size a known quantity rather than an open-ended one.

      PRIVACY. The document holds seat names, the cards and the arithmetic of
      the game, nothing else: no tokens, no e-mail addresses, no room codes.
      A `DEMO` seat's internal uid never leaves the game server. Account
      deletion NULLs the uid and the name of the leaving player's seats inside
      the JSON (`AccountDeletionService`), so a deleted account leaves the
      cards behind and takes the person with it.
    -->

    <changeSet id="2026-09-23-game-replays" author="mrodek">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <tableExists tableName="game_replays"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>

        <createTable tableName="game_replays">
            <column name="id" type="bigint" autoIncrement="true">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- One replay per recorded game; see the note above. -->
            <column name="game_result_id" type="bigint">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- The whole document (README §8.8). -->
            <column name="replay" type="jsonb">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- `@bela/bots`' BOT_VERSION, lifted out for grouping. -->
            <column name="bot_version" type="varchar(32)"/>
            <!-- Serialised size as sent, for growth monitoring. -->
            <column name="size_bytes" type="int"/>
            <column name="created_at" type="timestamptz" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </createTable>

        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="game_replays"
                             columnNames="game_result_id"
                             constraintName="uq_game_replays_game_result"/>

        <addForeignKeyConstraint baseTableName="game_replays"
                                 baseColumnNames="game_result_id"
                                 constraintName="fk_game_replays_game_result"
                                 referencedTableName="game_results"
                                 referencedColumnNames="id"
                                 onDelete="CASCADE"/>

        <!-- Both readers scan by age: the newest-first admin export and the
             nightly retention sweep. -->
        <createIndex tableName="game_replays" indexName="idx_game_replays_created_at">
            <column name="created_at"/>
        </createIndex>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
